<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#condolence-ceremony">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Condolence Ceremony</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Condolence Ceremony is the central ritual of the Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) Confederacy, held to mourn a dead chief and to raise up his successor. Set down in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law of Peace</a>, it includes words of comfort that wipe away grief, a recital of the founding chiefs' titles and the installation of the new chief. Its forms also opened treaty councils with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, French and English, and it is still performed today.</p></div>
